  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 AM EDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nA front currently arcs through the Ohio Valley crossing the \nMississippi and southern Plains and southward through central Texas. \nUpper level ridging is being compressed as the shortwave associated \nwith the front moves eastward towards our area. Surface high \npressure remains nearly anchored just off of the Mid-Atlantic coast.\n\nYesterday was our last dry day area-wide at least until next week. \nLater this morning, the portion of the front arcing through the Ohio \nValley will cross into western Tennessee. The morning will start \ndry, abnormally warm/humid. Chances for precipitation then begin to \nincrease as we get into the afternoon hours. The frontal system does \ncross the area sometime later tonight into early tomorrow, however, \nit will become stationary just to our south and not totally clear \nthe area until possibly late Sunday into early Monday. What this \nmeans will be periods of wet weather for the entire area this \nweekend. So far, a marginal risk for flash flooding is in place \nexcept for a majority of southwest Virginia. With the highest FFG \nacross our southern areas, some places could have the capacity to \ntake a good amount of rainfall and not have much of a threat unless \ntraining occurs with consecutive heavy showers/storms. Sunday still \nappears will have the heaviest rainfall amounts as the low center \nmoves overhead as the system finally pushes out. Forecast PWATs for \nSunday at this time do show values nearing or edging above 2 inches, \nwhich would be conducive for heavy rain makers.\n\nAs far as any storms, there isn't a severe threat, but there could \nbe some embedded storms. Instability overall will be lacking with \npoor lapse rates and shear. The greatest threat this weekend will be \nperiods of heavy rain with the possibility of localized flash \nflooding. Grand total QPF amounts, though being fine tuned with each \nnew forecast, show the entire forecast area seeing at least 1 inch \nof rain. Amounts of over 2 inches has been cut down as we've gotten \ncloser to the weekend, as well as any values above 3 inches. \nGreatest amounts are forecast south of I-40 and along the higher \nterrain of the Southern Appalachians.\n\nRain clears the region as we enter a new work week next week. A \nsecondary front will send everything out of here replacing it with \ndry weather, northerly flow, and temperatures typically recorded in \nearly October. Surface high pressure sinks in from the north and \nfrontal systems until the end of the period are forecast to remain \nwell to our south and to the north, for now. However, there's some \nuncertainty with regard to the southern extent of moisture and if it \nmay bring light precipitation to our southern areas.",
